course: dictionary
lesson: 12485
title: Formal words for edges
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are formal. You will meet them in books, on maps and in rules, and people often use a simpler word in everyday talk. Demarcate is one of these. «The white lines demarcate the football pitch.» The lines show exactly where the pitch starts and stops, so demarcate means to mark its edges clearly.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Lines, fences and borders all have edges, so demarcate must be about marking an edge or a limit. Try the swap test: «The white lines mark out the football pitch» still makes sense, so mark out is a good everyday meaning.
explain: Here are some more sentences: «A low wall demarcates the garden from the field.» «The treaty demarcated the border between the two countries.» In each one, something is shown clearly where one thing stops and another begins.
fact: Demarcate means to mark or set the edges or limits of something clearly.
fact: Demarcate is a formal word; in everyday talk people say «mark out» or «separate».
fact: Demarcate is a verb: demarcate, demarcates, demarcated, demarcating.
fact: «The white lines demarcate the pitch.» The lines show where the playing area starts and stops.
fact: Maps and treaties often demarcate the borders between countries.
exercise: «The white lines demarcate the netball court.» What does demarcate mean here? | To make it bigger | *To mark its edges clearly | To cover it with sand | To paint it a new colour
exercise: «A fence demarcates the edge of the farm.» Which everyday words could replace demarcates? | Cook up | Sing along | Jump over | *Mark out
exercise: «The treaty ___ the border last year.» Choose the correct form of demarcate for the past. | demarcates | demarcate | *demarcated | demarcating
test: «The rope demarcated the race track.» What does demarcated mean? | Pulled tight | *Marked the edges clearly | Cut into pieces | Tied in a knot
test: Which everyday phrase is closest in meaning to demarcate? | Shout loudly | *Separate | Fall asleep | Pass by quickly
exam: Which sentence uses demarcate correctly? | The lines demarcate my favourite song. | The lines demarcate a bowl of soup. | *The lines demarcate the edge of the playground. | The lines demarcate the sunny afternoon.
